<hr />
<h2 class="section-break">ABSTRACT</h2>
<p>The proliferation of digital misinformation, automated disinformation campaigns, and sensational clickbait represents a critical challenge to modern democratic discourse, financial stability, and public health. Conventional automated &quot;fake news&quot; detectors frequently suffer from three fundamental deficiencies: (1) <strong>overconfident binary classification</strong> (forcing arbitrary &quot;Real&quot; or &quot;Fake&quot; labels that miscategorize breaking news, satire, and legitimate opinion), (2) <strong>surface-level stylistic bias</strong> (relying purely on vocabulary frequencies without verifying real-world facts), and (3) <strong>black-box opacity</strong> (providing no auditable explanation of why an article was flagged).</p>
<p>This project presents <strong>Veritas</strong>, an end-to-end, multi-layered epistemic decision-support platform designed to transition misinformation detection from uncalibrated &quot;proof&quot; to transparent decision assistance. Veritas combines dual-track machine learning with factual claim corroboration:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Track 1 (Stylistic NLP):</strong> Employs calibrated classical models (TF-IDF vectorization with Linear Support Vector Machines, Logistic Regression, and Random Forest) paired with fine-tuned deep bidirectional transformers (<code>DistilBERT-base-uncased</code>) to assess lexical markers and emotional rhetoric.</li>
<li><strong>Track 2 (Empirical Corroboration):</strong> Features a proposition extraction engine that isolates discrete factual assertions and cross-references them against multi-source evidence registries, including the Google Fact Check Tools API, live public news feeds, and domain reputation classifications.</li>
<li><strong>Explainable AI (XAI):</strong> Implements token-level mathematical attribution heatmaps ($\text{Contribution}(t_i) = \text{TF-IDF}(t_i) \times w_i$) to visually show users exactly which lexical tokens swayed the classification.</li>
<li><strong>Multi-Signal Calibrated Synthesis:</strong> Formulates a three-class decision layer (<code>LIKELY_REAL</code>, <code>LIKELY_FAKE</code>, and <code>UNCERTAIN</code>) with dynamic Bayesian-style calibration that reconciles writing style with source domain accreditation.</li>
</ol>
<p>The system is delivered via an asynchronous FastAPI backend protected against Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF), a high-performance React 18 TypeScript web application with Three.js 3D visualizations, and a Google Chrome Manifest V3 browser extension for instantaneous in-browser page scanning. Comprehensive evaluation confirms test classification accuracy of $97.05%$ ($F_1 = 0.9714$, $\text{ROC-AUC} = 0.9965$), sub-2-second article ingestion, and seamless multilingual verification across English, Odia (ଓଡ଼ିଆ), Hindi (हिन्दी), and Spanish (Español).</p>
<p><strong>Keywords:</strong> Fake News Detection, Explainable AI (XAI), Natural Language Processing, DistilBERT, Epistemic Calibration, Proposition Extraction, Domain Forensics, FastAPI, React, SSRF Protection.</p>
