<h3>3.3 Mathematical and Algorithmic Formulations</h3>
<h4>3.3.1 TF-IDF Vectorization and Sparse Representation</h4>
<p>Given a document $d$ within a corpus $D$, the Term Frequency-Inverse Document Frequency weight of term $t$ is computed as:
$$\text{TF}(t, d) = \frac{f_{t, d}}{\sum_{t' \in d} f_{t', d}}$$
$$\text{IDF}(t, D) = \log\left(\frac{1 + |D|}{1 + |{d \in D : t \in d}|}\right) + 1$$
$$\text{TF-IDF}(t, d, D) = \text{TF}(t, d) \times \text{IDF}(t, D)$$
The resulting feature vectors are normalized using the Euclidean ($L_2$) norm:
$$v_{\text{norm}} = \frac{v}{|v|<em>2} = \frac{v}{\sqrt{\sum</em>{i=1}^{V} v_i^2}}$$
In Veritas, high-dimensional document vectors ($V = 10,000$ vocabulary terms) are stored using <strong>Compressed Sparse Row (<code>csr_matrix</code>)</strong> structures, reducing memory overhead from $O(N \times V)$ to $O(K)$ non-zero entries.</p>
<h4>3.3.2 Linear Classifiers and Probability Calibration</h4>
<p>For linear models, the decision function maps the sparse vector $x$ to a continuous margin:
$$z = w^T x + b = \sum_{j=1}^{V} w_j x_j + b$$
For Logistic Regression, the probability of an article being fake ($y=1$) is given by the standard sigmoid function:
$$P(y=1 \mid x) = \sigma(z) = \frac{1}{1 + e^{-z}}$$
For Linear Support Vector Classifiers (<code>LinearSVC</code>), Platt scaling or calibrated isotonic regression fits a calibrated mapping:
$$P(y=1 \mid x) = \frac{1}{1 + \exp(A \cdot z + B)}$$
where parameters $A$ and $B$ are estimated via cross-validation maximum likelihood.</p>
<h4>3.3.3 Token Attribution and Explainability Metric</h4>
<p>Explainable AI (XAI) feature attribution for linear models is formulated as:
$$\text{Contribution}(t_j) = x_j \cdot w_j$$</p>
<ul>
<li>$\text{Contribution}(t_j) &gt; 0$: The token pushed the prediction toward <code>LIKELY_FAKE</code> (sensationalism, hyperbolic framing).</li>
<li>$\text{Contribution}(t_j) &lt; 0$: The token pushed the prediction toward <code>LIKELY_REAL</code> (journalistic attribution, formal reporting verbs).
Tokens are ranked using argmax heap extraction in $O(V \log K)$ time complexity.</li>
</ul>
<h4>3.3.4 Proposition Importance Scoring Formula</h4>
<p>Extracted factual sentences are scored for epistemic importance $w_{\text{prop}} \in [0.25, 0.98]$ using:
$$w_{\text{prop}} = \min\left(0.98, \max\left(0.25, 0.30 + 0.20 \cdot \mathbb{I}<em>{\text{verb}} + 0.15 \cdot \mathbb{I}</em>{\text{num}} + 0.15 \cdot \mathbb{I}<em>{\text{official}} + 0.10 \cdot \mathbb{I}</em>{\text{quote}}\right)\right)$$
where indicator variables denote:</p>
<ul>
<li>$\mathbb{I}_{\text{verb}}$: Presence of reporting verbs (<em>stated, announced, confirmed, reported</em>).</li>
<li>$\mathbb{I}_{\text{num}}$: Presence of empirical quantities (<em>percentages, casualty counts, monetary amounts</em>).</li>
<li>$\mathbb{I}_{\text{official}}$: Named official institutions (<em>ministry, police, central bank, court</em>).</li>
<li>$\mathbb{I}_{\text{quote}}$: Direct attribution quotations (<em>&quot;...&quot;</em>).</li>
</ul>
<h4>3.3.5 Multi-Signal Decision Synthesis Formulation</h4>
<p>Let $P_{\text{fake}} \in [0, 1]$ represent the stylistic model prediction, $T_{\text{source}} \in [0, 1]$ represent the domain authority trust score, and $C_{\text{contra}} \in \mathbb{N}$ represent the count of contradicted claims. The calibrated confidence $C_{\text{final}}$ and final label $L_{\text{final}}$ are derived via:
$$C_{\text{final}} =
\begin{cases}
\min(0.92, \max(0.78, 0.35 \cdot (1 - P_{\text{fake}}) + 0.65 \cdot T_{\text{source}})) &amp; \text{if } T_{\text{source}} \ge 0.75 \text{ and } C_{\text{contra}} = 0 \
\min(0.95, \max(0.75, P_{\text{fake}} + 0.15)) &amp; \text{if } T_{\text{source}} \le 0.30 \text{ or } C_{\text{contra}} &gt; 0 \
\max(P_{\text{fake}}, 1 - P_{\text{fake}}) &amp; \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}$$</p>
<p>$$L_{\text{final}} =
\begin{cases}
\text{LIKELY_REAL} &amp; \text{if } T_{\text{source}} \ge 0.75 \text{ and } C_{\text{contra}} = 0 \text{ and } P_{\text{fake}} \le 0.60 \
\text{LIKELY_FAKE} &amp; \text{if } C_{\text{contra}} &gt; 0 \text{ or } (T_{\text{source}} \le 0.30 \text{ and } P_{\text{fake}} \ge 0.50) \
\text{UNCERTAIN} &amp; \text{if margin } |P_{\text{fake}} - 0.50| &lt; 0.20 \text{ without established source override}
\end{cases}$$</p>
<hr />
<h3>3.4 Data Flow and Interaction Modeling</h3>
<p>The interaction lifecycle follows an asynchronous sequence:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Request Ingestion:</strong> The client transmits a payload (URL or text string) to <code>/api/v1/analyze-url</code> or <code>/api/v1/verify</code>.</li>
<li><strong>SSRF Validation:</strong> The server validates hostnames, resolving DNS records to ensure no destination IP belongs to private subnet blocks (<code>127.0.0.1</code>, <code>10.0.0.0/8</code>, <code>192.168.0.0/16</code>, <code>169.254.169.254</code>).</li>
<li><strong>Extraction &amp; NLP:</strong> <code>fetch_article_from_url()</code> retrieves the HTML, extracts metadata, strips noise elements, and normalizes text.</li>
<li><strong>Dual Inference:</strong> Stylistic classifiers compute class probabilities and extract token-level attributions.</li>
<li><strong>Corroboration:</strong> Factual claims are prioritized, stance checks are executed, and domain authority is queried.</li>
<li><strong>Synthesis &amp; Storage:</strong> <code>synthesize_decision()</code> resolves the multi-signal verdict, persists the relational records, and returns the response contract to the UI.</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<h3>3.5 Database and Relational Entity Design</h3>
<p>The database layer utilizes SQLAlchemy 2.0 ORM with relational cascades:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong><code>User</code> (1) $\rightarrow$ <code>Analysis</code> (Many):</strong> Tracks ownership of verification reports.</li>
<li><strong><code>Analysis</code> (1) $\rightarrow$ <code>Claim</code> (Many):</strong> Preserves isolated propositions per report.</li>
<li><strong><code>Claim</code> (1) $\rightarrow$ <code>Evidence</code> (Many):</strong> Retains external fact-check citations per claim.</li>
</ul>
